add('7cfd90',4,'Combinatorics',['pigeonhole principle','normalization'],'Shift the smallest element to one, then pair the remaining allowed positions.',r'For integer $k>1$, choose $k+2$ distinct numbers from $\{1,\ldots,3k\}$. Prove two have difference strictly between $k$ and $2k$.')
add('7e91e4',4,'Algebra',['complex roots','conjugation'],'Conjugation pairs powers of a, restricting a nonreal root to third or fourth roots of unity.',r'For which $a\in\mathbb C$ does $(x-a)(x-a^2)(x-a^3)$ have only real coefficients?')
add('8808e0',3,'Algebra',['floor function','divisibility'],'Equality between a sum and the sum of its floors forces two integrality conditions.')
add('ac1823',6,'Combinatorics',['invariants','alternating sums'],'Parity settles the first target; an alternating sum rules out one operation before a second residue-class invariant is used.',r'On a circle are 300 integers, initially one 1 and 299 zeros. A move either simultaneously replaces each entry by itself minus its two neighbours, or adds the same $+1$ or $-1$ to two entries three positions apart. Can one reach (a) two consecutive ones and zeros elsewhere; (b) three consecutive ones and zeros elsewhere?')
add('be7b91',5.5,'Geometry',['similarity','coordinates'],'A circle equation fixes the auxiliary point; coordinates then prove both parallelism and concurrency.',r'In $ABC$, suppose $AB=AC$ and $\angle A<60^\circ$. Choose $D\in AC$ with $\angle DBC=\angle A$. The perpendicular bisector of $BD$ meets the parallel to $BC$ through $A$ at $E$. Choose $F$ on ray $CA$ beyond $A$ with $AF=2AC$. Prove $BE\parallel AC$, and that the perpendiculars from $F$ to $AB$ and from $E$ to $AC$ meet on $BD$.',r'Normalize $B=(-1,0)$, $C=(1,0)$, $A=(0,h)$, where $h>\sqrt3$. Similarity of $BCD$ and $ABC$ gives $BD=BC=2$. Writing $D=(t,h(1-t))$ in the circle equation $BD=2$ gives $t=(h^2-3)/(h^2+1)$ (the other root is $C$). Write $E=(e,h)$. The equation $EB^2=ED^2$ simplifies to $2e+(1-t)(1+h^2)=0$, so $e=-2$. Thus $\overrightarrow{BE}=(-1,h)=\overrightarrow{CA}$. Also $F=(-2,3h)$. The two requested perpendiculars are $y-3h=-(x+2)/h$ and $y-h=(x+2)/h$; they meet at $T=(h^2-2,2h)$. Now $\overrightarrow{BT}=(h^2-1,2h)$ is proportional to $\overrightarrow{BD}=\frac{2}{h^2+1}(h^2-1,2h)$, proving the concurrency.')
add('cd4a7a',6.5,'Number theory',['functional equations','arbitrarily large primes'],'Subtract adjacent values and vary one input to obtain infinitely many prime divisors of a fixed difference.',r'Find $f:\mathbb N^2\to\mathbb N$ such that $f(a,b)+a+b=f(a,1)+f(1,b)+ab$ for positive $a,b$, and every odd prime dividing $(a+b)(a+b-1)$ also divides $f(a,b)$.')
add('17e870',5.5,'Combinatorics',['triple systems','double counting'],'Parity and edge counts exclude four sizes; explicit finite-plane constructions establish the other two.',r'Call $n$ admissible if the edges of the complete graph on $n$ vertices can be coloured so that every edge belongs to exactly one monochromatic triangle. Which of $7,8,9,10,11,12$ are admissible?',r'''Every edge belongs to a unique monochromatic triangle, so these triangles partition the edges. At a fixed vertex they pair the other vertices; hence $n$ is odd. Counting edges gives $3\mid n(n-1)/2$, eliminating 11 as well as the even values.

For $n=7$, label vertices by the seven nonzero binary triples. For each distinct $x,y$, group the three edges joining $x,y,x+y$, with coordinates added modulo two; assign each such triangle its own colour. Every pair belongs to exactly one of these triangles.

For $n=9$, label vertices by pairs of residues modulo three. The lines of the $3\times3$ affine plane (three horizontal, three vertical, and three in each of the two diagonal directions) are triples. Every pair lies on exactly one line. Give the three edges of each line its own colour. Thus precisely $7$ and $9$ work.''')
add('38e372',5.5,'Combinatorics',['geometric counting','induction'],'Remove a convex-hull point and bound new separations by lines through it; convex position attains the recurrence.',r'A section of a finite planar point set is an unordered partition into two subsets separable by a line avoiding all the points; an empty subset is allowed. What is the greatest number of sections for $n$ points?')
add('523c4a',4.5,'Number theory',['multiplicative order','smallest prime divisor'],'The least prime divisor forces the multiplicative order to be one, contradicting the linear modulus.',r'Find positive integers $n$ such that $(n^{3n-2}-3n+1)/(3n-2)$ is an integer.')
add('63e1eb',4,'Geometry',['angle bisectors','cyclic quadrilaterals'],'Each of the four intersections lies on the circle with diameter BC after one auxiliary cyclic quadrilateral.',r'The incircle of $ABC$ touches $AB,AC$ at $D,E$, and the $A$-excircle touches rays $AB,AC$ at $F,G$. The internal angle bisectors at $B,C$ meet $DE$ at $X,Y$; their external angle bisectors meet $FG$ at $Z,W$. Prove $X,Y,Z,W$ concyclic.')
add('6415a1',5.5,'Number theory',['multiplicative functions','induction'],'Parity-dependent sum-of-two-squares identities permit strong induction without assuming complete multiplicativity.',r'Let $f:\mathbb N\to\mathbb N$ be multiplicative on coprime arguments, with $f(4)=4$ and $f(m^2+n^2)=f(m^2)+f(n^2)$ for positive $m,n$. Prove $f(m^2)=m^2$ for every positive $m$.')
add('6f9827',7,'Geometry',['reflection','spiral similarity'],'Reflecting an interior point creates circumcentres; two linked similarities establish concurrence on the reflected-point circle.',r'Reflect an interior point $T$ of $ABC$ in $BC,CA,AB$ to obtain $A_1,B_1,C_1$. The lines $A_1T,B_1T,C_1T$ meet $(A_1B_1C_1)$ again at $A_2,B_2,C_2$. Prove $AA_2,BB_2,CC_2$ concur on that circle.')
add('7234cc',5.5,'Number theory',['parameter choices','primes'],'Two carefully chosen substitutions make the quotient k and k+1; a remaining small case and a bound finish.',r'Find positive integers $k$ such that $(m+n)/(m^2-kmn+n^2)$ never equals a composite integer for positive integers $m,n$ with nonzero denominator.')
add('77ac9e',2,'Algebra',['completing the square'],'Complete the square in a+b to obtain the minimum and its equality case.')
add('854625',6.5,'Combinatorics',['partitions','generating functions'],'Count singleton part sizes with a marked generating function, whose cancellation matches the count of parts of size two.',r'Let $K_n=\{(a_1,\ldots,a_n)\in\mathbb Z_{\ge0}^n:\sum ja_j=n\}$. Write $J(a)=|\{j:a_j=1\}|$. Prove $\sum_{a\in K_n}J(a)=\sum_{a\in K_{n+1}}a_2$.',r'Work with formal power series and put $P(t)=\prod_{j\ge1}(1-t^j)^{-1}$. Marking a coordinate $a_j=1$ contributes $P(t)t^j(1-t^j)$, since its original factor is replaced by $t^j$. Therefore the generating series of the left side is $$P(t)\sum_{j\ge1}(t^j-t^{2j})=P(t)\frac{t}{1-t^2}.$$ Counting coordinate $a_2$ with multiplicity gives $$P(t)(1-t^2)\sum_{k\ge0}kt^{2k}=P(t)\frac{t^2}{1-t^2}.$$ The second series is $t$ times the first. Comparing coefficients of $t^{n+1}$ proves the identity. Every coefficient involves finitely many terms, so these formal manipulations are valid.')
add('9e1bcd',4,'Number theory',['gcd','parity'],'Reduce by the gcd, then prove two linear forms are coprime using the parity condition.',r'For positive integers $m,n$ of opposite parity, prove $(3m^2+5mn)/(3n^2+mn)$ is not an integer.')
